      "role": "user",
      "content": "That \"statistical illusion\" angle is a good start. If I use that, I need to be able to defend it if the Board's data analyst starts digging. \n\nSpecifically, they're going to point out that a 12% drop over 24 hours is a pretty massive \"blip\" for a demographic this size. To make the \"noise\" argument stick, I need some sort of qualitative or secondary metric to back it up. \n\nIs there anything in the deeper sentiment breakdown—like how these voters are actually discussing the issue—that would support the idea that this is just a reaction to the specific phrasing of the tax reform clip rather than a fundamental shift in their values? I need a way to prove that the core of the suburban support is actually still intact.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
